Andreas Scrosati is leaving Got Talent and Baywatch producer Fremantle after eight years to launch "a new entrepreneurial venture."

The exec will stay in his role as Fremantle Group COO and CEO of Continental Europe until the end of January 2027. Announcement of his exit comes three days after Deadline revealed Fremantle U.S. COO Suzanne Lopez was leaving after 23 years.

Scrosati has been with Fremantle since 2018, having joined from Sky Italia. Initially group COO, he added Continential Europe to his duties in 2021. Notably, he led Fremantle's M&A strategy and led the acquisitions of Poor Things producer Element Pictures, Asacha Media Group and Costiera maker Lux Video. He also backed pushes into the FAST channel business and AI, notably spearheading the creation Imaginae Studios, whose first production, Art Awakens , launched this year. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article1-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid","mid-articleX","mid-article","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[2,2],[300,250],[620,350],[300,251],[501,282],[3,3],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories News 'American Idol' Gets Rid Of Hollywood Week In Favor Of More Live Shows As ABC Music Competition Moves To Georgia: "Major Changes Coming" Executives Fremantle U.S. COO Suzanne Lopez Exiting After Over 20 Years

The Italian exec also backed Fremantle's push into global film, with the company delivering its 100th feature this year. The strategy was expensive and in March, Fremantle parent RTL Group announced Fremantle would no longer fully finance films following a review. Watch on Deadline if ( !window.pmc.harmony?.isEventAdScheduledTime() ) { pmcCnx.cmd.push(function() { pmcCnx({ settings: { plugins: { pmcAtlasMG: { iabPlcmt: 2, } } }, playerId: '32fe25c4-79aa-406a-af44-69b41e969e71', playlistId: '0199e506-e642-7728-8264-463efe71337b', }).render("connatix_contextual_player_div"); }); } else { // This should only be get called when page cache is not cleared and it's event time. window.pmc.harmony?.switchToHarmonyPlayer(); }

Scrosati can point to four Academy Awards and 17 Academy nominations, the Golden Lion at Venice and awards in Cannes and Berlin as an answer to Fremantle's critics.

&#x201C;Andrea has made an enormous contribution to Fremantle over the past eight years," said Fremantle CEO Jennifer Mullin. "He has been instrumental in our growth and transformation, from helping build Fremantle into a true portfolio company and leading our M&A strategy, to establishing our film strategy and constantly pushing us to think differently about innovation and the future of our business.

"Just as importantly, Andrea has been a trusted colleague, partner and a great friend to me. I will miss working alongside him enormously, but I&#x2019;m incredibly excited for him as he takes this next entrepreneurial step. I&#x2019;m grateful that he has agreed to stay with us through the end of January to support the transition, and I know everyone at Fremantle joins me in wishing him every success in this new adventure." blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dl-mid-article-inject2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article2","mid-articleX","mid-article","mid","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#x201C;These eight years at Fremantle have been an extraordinary privilege," added Scrosati. "Fremantle is a unique company, with incredible people, some of the world&#x2019;s finest creative talent and an exceptional portfolio of IP. I leave with enormous pride in what we have achieved together and great affection for the company and everyone who makes it what it is. The time feels right for me to take the next step and focus on building something new.

"I want to thank Jen for her leadership, vision and trust, all my colleagues across Fremantle, and RTL Group and Bertelsmann for their unwavering support over the years. Fremantle has an exciting future ahead, and I look forward to working with the team over the coming months to ensure a smooth transition.&#x201D;

New season, new case, new lies. The Apple TV legal anthology thriller &#x201C; Presumed Innocent &#x201D; is back for Season 2 on Feb. 19.

This time, Rachel Brosnahan and Matthew Rhys are taking the stand. A 50-second teaser released by Apple shows Brosnahan, Rhys, Courtney B. Vance and Fiona Shaw in the tense moments of a trial, while all being asked the same question, &#x201C;Do you swear to tell the truth, the whole truth and nothing but the truth, so help you God?,&#x201D; before the swelling soundtrack drowns out the oath, building until Jack Reynor&#x2019;s character proclaims, &#x201C;I do.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Season 2 is based on &#x201C;Dissection of a Murder,&#x201D; the novel by Jo Murray, which follows the up-and-coming defense attorney Lelia Reynolds (Brosnahan) as she faces off against her district attorney husband (Rhys) in a murder case that threatens to destroy both their careers and their marriage.

The first season, released in 2024, starred Jake Gyllenhaal and Ruth Negga as the prosecutor Rusty Sabich and his wife, Barbara Sabich. The eight-episode series received four Emmy nominations, two of which were for Gyllenhaal and Negga&#x2019;s performances.

The first two episodes of Season 2 will drop on Feb. 19 followed by weekly releases until April 2. David E. Kelley and J.J. Abrams helm the series. Kelley serves as showrunner on Season 2 and executive produces through David E. Kelley Productions in association with Warner Bros. Television alongside Matthew Tinker. Abrams executive produces through Bad Robot Productions. Rachel Rusch Rich, Dee Johnson, Dustin Thomason and Brosnahan also serve as executive producers. Gyllenhaal executive produces under his Nine Stories banner. Murray and Scott Turow, author of &#x201C;Presumed Innocent,&#x201D; are co-executive producers. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&nbsp; Share on X (Opens in new window) X Share on Facebook (Opens in new window) Facebook Add Us On Google Known for her stint on Captain Marvel and Wonder Woman Historia: The Amazons , the latter of which won her an Eisner Award, Kelly Sue DeConnick is no stranger to feminist comics. Although her indie work may be less known, it is hardly of lesser quality. Her collaboration with Valentine de Landro, Bitch Planet , may just be the perfect source material for the next sci-fi adaptation to be made from a comic.&nbsp;

Bitch Planet ran for ten issues between 2014 and 2017, with a follow-up anthology series called Bitch Planet: Triple Feature , which ran for five issues. Each Triple Feature contained three stories from different writers and artists, including many of the top creatives in the industry today. Such creatives included Matt Fraction, the current writer on Batman ; Che Grayson, who co-writes Absolute Catwoman and wrote for the Absolute Cassandra Cain one-shot ; and Vita Ayala, known for their work on New Mutants during the Krakoan X-Men era.&nbsp;

Bitch Planet technically never ended. Both DeConnick and de Landro have expressed wanting to go back to it, but never quite found the right time . The themes of incarceration and feminism only got more and more relevant over the years, and DeConnick has mentioned at conventions that the book is hitting a little too close to home. This relevance — in the wake of television successes like Orange Is the New Black, The Handmaid’s Tale , and the follow-up The Testaments — makes Bitch Planet perfect for adaptation.&nbsp;

Bitch Planet takes place in a dystopian reality where not only are non-compliant women sent to prison, but they are also sent to prison in space. Crudely referred to as Bitch Planet both in-universe and in the comics’ title, the Auxiliary Compliance Outpost is exactly what readers would expect.&nbsp;

DeConnick created Bitch Planet out of a love for the 1960s and 1970s’ women's prison movies and wanted to spotlight the intriguing and difficult aspects of women’s lives in prison. Nudity is common throughout the comic, but it isn’t meant to be cheesecakey or serve as eye candy. Instead, it’s meant to help flesh out these characters. One of the most striking moments from the first issue of Bitch Planet is the leader of Auxiliary Compliance Outpost explaining that the prison jumpsuits are one-size-fits-all when that clearly isn’t the case. One woman struggles to put on, given that it barely has room for just one of her tits.&nbsp;

Prison media in general is packed with existential dread — prisoners are forcibly removed from society, stripped of their rights and belongings, and often feel trapped. However, Bitch Planet takes it to a whole new level by transporting them to a different planet. A husband of one of the prisoners, Maria Collins, explains that people never hear of escapees from Bitch Planet , emphasizing the prison as a dead end. Besides, even if they did escape, where would they go?&nbsp;

Shows like Orange Is the New Black — which the creators avoided watching in fear of taking too much inspiration from — inevitably allow for reintegration into society by the end. Such a fate doesn’t seem possible for the protagonists of Bitch Planet. They’ve committed too many trespasses, whether it be gluttony, pride, weakness, or wickedness. The women are seen as beyond correction, and it is for the good of Father Earth — an interesting subversion given how Earth is typically referred to as a Mother — that they are sent away, lest a sickness spread.&nbsp;

As written in the afterword of the first issue of Bitch Planet , “the most striking thing about Bitch Planet is that we’re already on it.” Bitch Planet was unafraid of social commentary, both in and out of its story, remarking on not only sexism, but racism, fatphobia, and the intersections that they cross. Bitch Planet uses a decoy protagonist in Marian Collins, a middle-aged white woman who became so “non-compliant” that her husband was driven to an affair. The comic quickly kills her off and pivots to Penelope. It pivots to a fat, black woman, emphasizing how African American women are 3x more likely to be incarcerated.&nbsp;

Penelope may not be the standard sci-fi protagonist, but that’s part of why she works. She’s the type of woman that the prison wardens expect to be able to break easily, but they can’t. At one point in the comic, she’s forced to look into a mirror that’s built to show her true ideal self. The wardens assume it’ll show a thin, beautiful, and compliant woman. To their chagrin, however, it shows Penelope exactly as she is. Penelope is happy with herself, a crime that gets her sentenced to the Auxiliary Compliance Outpost.&nbsp;

Turning Bitch Planet into a television show allows room not only for the stellar characterization that DeConnick and de Landro utilized, but also gives space for the gravity of the worldbuilding. What does it mean to dedicate an entire planet to a women’s prison? The leader of the Auxiliary Compliance Outpost is the face of a woman that all the prisoners hate upon seeing. The Handmaid’s Tale showed one rationale behind a woman punishing other women with Serena Joy. What other reasons might there be? Ultimately, this comic is not getting any less relevant with time, and it still has the ability to say something that not every show on TV does.&nbsp;

What other comics have potential for great sci-fi dystopia TV? Comment down below!

