As NBC celebrates its 100th anniversary this year, the company has been recognizing some of its most memorable stars, TV shows, producers and even executives. But NBC couldn&#x2019;t run without some of its unsung heroes &#x2014; the staffers who do the day-to-day work behind the scenes, usually with very little fanfare.

So that's what Variety is doing here: We found six staffers from throughout NBC who keep the network humming. These are their stories. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Royal Family Embraces Robert Pattinson's 007 Casting: ‘Welcome to His Majesty’s Secret Service, Mr. Bond’ Jeff Bezos Was 'Heavily Involved' in Casting Robert Pattinson as James Bond and Admits Initial Fears 'Because He's Already So Famous'

Dawn DiCicco (Associate Director, &#x201C;NBC Nightly News&#x201D;)

Dawn DiCicco&#x2019;s job description as associate director of &#x201C;NBC Nightly News&#x201D; wouldn&#x2019;t exactly fit neatly on a LinkedIn page.

&#x201C;My job is to know everything about everything all the time. I&#x2019;m responsible for putting out fires before they start,&#x201D; she says. &#x201C;It&#x2019;s to basically gap fill every sort of gap and keep an eye on everything that goes on air. A lot of my job is anticipating people&#x2019;s needs and what they&#x2019;re going to want before they can even think that they&#x2019;re going to ask for that, so I can already have it ready to go. I&#x2019;m the final line of defense.&#x201D;

Every day is different on &#x201C;Nightly News.&#x201D; Depending on how and when news breaks, the show can air up to six times per day. That means moving quickly and decisively on what news is most worthy of inclusion in the 30-minute telecast. A natural disaster may replace a courtroom trial verdict, a financial free fall in Asia might be inserted into the broadcast and a political brouhaha taken out. It&#x2019;s a blizzard of news and decision-making.

&#x201C;For me, those days are the most fun," she adds. "I love the days where like I walk in here and I have zero idea what&#x2019;s going to happen. The days where when your whole show gets blown up by, like some kind of crazy verdict or some news breaking. That&#x2019;s where I thrive best because I like to think if you can work under pressure like that, you can work and do anything.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Favorite NBC show of all time: &#x201C;The Traitors&#x201D;

Jeanette Eliot (Senior Director, Events and Special Projects)

When &#x201C;Saturday Night Live&#x201D; was celebrating its 50th anniversary a couple of years ago, lots of people went into action &#x2014; Lorne Michaels, Adam Sandler &#x2026; and Jeanette Eliot. Eliot was tasked with ordering 6,000 pairs of Converse sneakers for NBC employees, given as a gift to celebrate the occasion.

&#x201C;I got to do that,&#x201D; Eliot says. &#x201C;I wanted to make that happen.&#x201D;

Eliot, who stared her career as an NBC temp in 1995, has made a lot of things happen over her career. She's the person who fulfills orders for promotional mailers that go to press, selects items and keepsakes for hundreds of events (including premieres and internal meetings), and finds creative ways to celebrate milestones.

Eliot often finds herself starting her day before the sun rises because of the need to deal with retailers from faraway time zones. But she takes enormous pride in getting the work done &#x2014; no matter the obstacles faced.

&#x201C;I&#x2019;m very hard on myself,&#x201D; she says. I think my biggest challenge is just not disappointing anybody. I&#x2019;ll always figure out a way to do it.&#x201D;

A recent project included ordering NBC 100 calendars from vendor on the other side of the world. Making things extra tricky: They had to arrive in time so she could send to press by a specific date.

&#x201C;I didn&#x2019;t think they were going to make it in time, and when I saw the boxes arrive here from China, it literally brought tears to my eyes," she says.

Favorite NBC show of all time: &#x201C;American Ninja Warrior&#x201D;

Evan Klupt (Senior Director, &#x201C;Today&#x201D; Production Operations)

When viewers turn on &#x201C;Today&#x201D; each morning, they see a well-lit set, smiling and well-groomed anchors, reporters around the world and a production team that knows the right buttons to push as the two-hour telecast moves along. What they don&#x2019;t see is Evan Klupt.

Klupt, who has been at NBC since 1995, is among the many &#x201C;Today&#x201D; professionals who makes sure the morning program looks like a well-oiled machine. He oversees the show&#x2019;s production management team that handles everything from setting up live remotes on location, guest logistics and prepping those guests to go on air, among lots of other things.

&#x201C;My job touches all areas of that production, whether it&#x2019;s in the studio or on the Plaza in Rockefeller Center,&#x201D; Klupt says. &#x201C;It&#x2019;s dealing with personnel, hair, makeup, wardrobe, green rooms, concerts and large-scale productions. That&#x2019;s kind of my beat.&#x201D;

Among his NBC highlights are working at 13 Olympics (he met his wife at the 2000 Sydney Games) and traveling the globe. By his count, he&#x2019;s now worked in 50 countries. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

A recent &#x201C;Today&#x201D; trip to Ireland put everything into perspective. "We had the show that we did there recently and I was thinking that when viewers tune into something, they only see the anchors," he says. "They see a beautiful set with an amazing rainbow going across the set into the skyline of Ireland, but they&#x2019;re not seeing everything that went into it &#x2014; procuring the table they&#x2019;re sitting at, getting the flowers on the desk, shipping hundreds of cases of equipment and dealing with customs, logistics, hotels and transportation. Our group does everything from the very beginning.&#x201D;

Favorite NBC show of all time: &#x201C;Seinfeld&#x201D;

Justin Michael Ratchford (Field Producer, &#x201C;Dateline NBC&#x201D;)

If Justin Michael Ratchford hadn&#x2019;t gone into long-form journalism on &#x201C;Dateline NBC,&#x201D; he might have had another career as a therapist. That&#x2019;s because in his role as an &#x201C;Dateline NBC&#x201D; field producer, he often needs to create a comfort zone with those subjects who are about to divulge their lives in front of the camera.

&#x201C;We have to build those relationships and foster them, to get someone willing to talk,&#x201D; Ratchford says. &#x201C;I typically say, &#x2018;I&#x2019;m going to ask you about the worst moments of your life.&#x2019;&#x201D;

Not only does Ratchford have to develop a feel for how a prospective interviewee will share their story, he also must do it while juggling several areas of production on multiple episodes at once. Putting one story together, or even a bunch of them, is not a linear process.

&#x201C;A story takes as long as it needs,&#x201D; Ratchford says. &#x201C;We had one story that was started in 2007 and didn&#x2019;t air until 2021.&#x201D;

"Dateline" has also inspired him to explore more avenues in storytelling. &#x201C;I&#x2019;m a huge film buff,&#x201D; he says. &#x201C;That&#x2019;s what really made me fall in love with this job. Movies and films were my first love. I always said I want to shoot something and see it on a big screen.&#x201D;

Favorite NBC show of all time: &#x201C;The Fresh Prince of Bel-Air&#x201D;

Jessica Sheeley-Saunders (Vice President, Productions and Operations, Specials and Live Events)

Everyone loves a parade. But maybe not as much as Jessica Sheeley-Saunders. In her job working with specials and live events, Sheeley-Saunders says it&#x2019;s her job to &#x201C;connect all the dots between all the departments. It&#x2019;s a bit like crisis management in solving problems and, a like the mom, making sure everyone&#x2019;s doing what they&#x2019;re supposed to be doing.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid3' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Sheeley-Saunders, who joined NBC three years ago, says she feels honored to be in a position to bring some of the country&#x2019;s most iconic events &#x2014; like the Macy&#x2019;s Thanksgiving Day Parade, the Christmas tree-lighting at Rockefeller Center and this year, the Emmy Awards &#x2014; and make sure it all goes smoothly.

&#x201C;In this position, I get to be a part of some of America&#x2019;s favorite traditions, and it&#x2019;s just a little surreal,&#x201D; she says. "There are 40 million people watching the parade, and I get to be in the front row, making that happen.&#x201D;

Calling herself &#x201C;a proud San Fernando Valley girl, born and raised&#x201D; and graduate of Cal State Northridge, Sheeley-Saunders says she looks forward to being on the streets of New York every year in late November.

&#x201C;To stand there on Thanksgiving morning and see the entire city shut down and watch these giant balloons go by &#x2026; There was a moment when I called my husband and said, &#x2018;I made the right decision. I am so happy that I said yes to this.&#x2019;&#x201D;

Favorite NBC show of all time: &#x201C;Friends&#x201D;

Francine Spray (Senior Vice President, Events and Special Projects, Universal Television Entertainment)

Francine Spray joined NBC as a Page in 1988, and has been there for some of its most landmark moments. That's because she's the person charged with making sure events go off smoothly, as sheand her team oversee approximately 200 events a year that the company schedules.

Spray is tasked with bringing together premieres, screenings, off-site meetings, press events, receptions, red carpets and more from inception to completion. She knows the best restaurants in Los Angeles to hold an event, the amount of alcohol necessary for a post-Golden Globes soir&#xE9;e and how a post-event party for NBC brass at the Paris Olympics needs to be organized differently than for those Games held in Seoul, Barcelona or Athens.

Spray notes that her job has changed over the years, as the reliance on social media grew in getting promotion and a good word out for the company&#x2019;s properties and programming.

&#x201C;We now need to plan for social and Instagrammable moments,&#x201D; Spray says. &#x201C;It&#x2019;s really a big part of what we do. We have to have a photo moment at every event.&#x201D;

Spray and her team had to make a huge pivot during COVID, when people weren&#x2019;t allowed to gather. She says it was "one of our most transformative times for this department… We had to get hooked on to Zoom in two seconds flat. That was the only way we kept ourselves going from the PR perspective &#x2014; to do all of those virtual junkets and keep the publicity machine rolling, so to speak.&#x201D;

Favorite NBC show of all time: &#x201C;Hill Street Blues&#x201D;

If there is one thing Candle Media founder Kevin Mayer understands, it is mergers. The exec oversaw Disney &#x2019;s 2019 acquisition of 21st Century Fox during his term as the company&#x2019;s chief strategy officer, where he was also involved in Disney&#x2019;s acquisition of Pixar, Marvel, Lucasfilm and BAMTech.

During a keynote talk at the Doha Industry Days , Mayer reflected on the industry&#x2019;s recent wave of mergers, most notably with Skydance . &#x201C;Revenue isn&#x2019;t continuing to grow the way it was 10 years ago,&#x201D; he said. &#x201C;That usually indicates that most ecosystems need to consolidate. You&#x2019;ve seen that corporately already happen, obviously, with the combination of Warner Brothers and Paramount and Skydance.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Benjamín Vicuña, Star of Netflix Hit Series 'Envious,' Joins ‘My Last Stand' by Goya-Winner Matías Bize 'Woman Unknown' Breakout Mathilde Arcel on Her Venice Award-Winning Performance and Life After Her Lido Triumph: 'Everything Is Open'

&#x201C;You see NBCUniversal not only being split off of Comcast but itself being split between cable networks and the rest of the studio and the rest of the streaming business,&#x201D; he added. &#x201C;Those splits are intended to ultimately lead to consolidation of those two companies with other companies. So you are seeing this urge to merge driven by basic financial dynamics, and that is: when growth slows, you want to take that revenue base and amortize it over a smaller cost base.&#x201D; This movement, Mayer added, will be an &#x201C;ongoing process for the next several years.&#x201D;

Still weighing in on Hollywood&#x2019;s merger fever, Mayer looked back at his own experience at Disney, noting how &#x201C;easy&#x201D; it is to &#x201C;mess up an acquisition.&#x201D; Bringing two companies together is a process that needs to be done &#x201C;very carefully,&#x201D; he said, recalling when Disney bought Pixar from under the realm of former CEO Steve Jobs in 2006. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#x201C;Jobs was a very loud voice on this one,&#x201D; he noted, going on to tell the audience how, from his very first meeting with the Apple founder, former Disney CEO Bob Iger went straight into ironing out how to avoid the possible &#x201C;cultural issues&#x201D; of the merger.

&#x201C;Those cultural issues ended up being extremely important,&#x201D; he said. &#x201C;Thank God he brought that up and thank God he was so focused on that because that really did drive our decision-making. When we bought Pixar, we negotiated with the team and Steve Jobs that they'd be kept separate. Creatively, they'd keep their campus up in Northern California even though they were gonna be highly involved in the running of Disney animation down in Los Angeles.&#x201D;

The same strategy was adopted in Disney&#x2019;s high-profile acquisition of Marvel. &#x201C;The last thing you want to do is take the very thing you bought and ruin it by bringing it into an existing organization like the Walt Disney Company Studios and sit on them creatively,&#x201D; he pointed out. &#x201C;It's much, much better to leave those creative folks to flourish.&#x201D;

&#x201C;Integration, careful integration, thoughtful integration, and sometimes not integrating on the creative side, that's crucial to make these things work,&#x201D; he continued. &#x201C;It's hard to do because when you buy a company, you think, &#x2018;Hey, I just paid a lot of money. We put ourselves on the line here. We want to bring that in and really manage carefully.&#x2019; But you have to work against that instinct, and that's where Bob Iger was a genius with that.&#x201D;

Mayer also spoke about his experience acquiring labels under his Candle Media realm, most notably the company&#x2019;s 2021 acquisition of Moonbug Entertainment, the parent company of children&#x2019;s phenomenon &#x201C;CoComelon.&#x201D; Moonbug had previously bought CoComelon itself in 2020. &#x201C;We were very successful with that,&#x201D; said the exec of the strategy of growing &#x201C;CoComelon,&#x201D; with the YouTube children&#x2019;s franchise surpassing 200 million subscribers and ranking between 4 to 13 billion monthly views. &#x201C;CoComelon&#x201D; also surpassed artists like Taylor Swift and Bad Bunny in streaming numbers in 2025.

